the complex relationship between master  chief and his ai partner cortana has  always been one of the strongest driving  forces for the halo franchise it's a  genuine bond but one born out of  manipulation as cortana is gifted to  chief as a perfect companion to ensure  he maintains peak efficiency as a super  soldier in this way cortana has always  been the more dominant voice in the  partnership she tells chief where to go  and what to do and chief  single-mindedness coupled with the  ability to solely rely on her means he  never has to develop emotionally healthy  bonds with normal human beings halo  infant is the first time we've seen the  power dynamics of chief's relationship  with an ai flipped creating an  intriguing new take for spartan 117  paired with a new ai simply named the  weapon chief takes on a more fatherly  figure in infinite where cortana is  assertive quick-witted and mature the  weapon is awkward overly trusting and  silly she sounds and acts like a  child-like version of cortana pushing  chief into taking on a more domineering  role that has far more control over her  agency this opens new opportunities for  a halo story in a franchise that has  long had an undercurrent theme of  familial connections in motherhood  infinite is the first in the series to  focus its narrative around familial  responsibility and fatherhood the  strength of that narrative alongside the  open world and new traversal mechanics  delivers a welcoming freshness to the  two decades old series  [Music]  other than chief halo infinite doesn't  have much in the way familiar faces very  early into the campaign you're  confronted with the startling reveal  that a lot of the folks you've come to  know over previous games are dead or  missing in action chief finds himself on  a new halo ring named zeta halo which  has been partially destroyed the  banished an independent army of multiple  alien species that splintered off of the  covenant want the ring for some unknown  purpose and she fails to stop them  without any of his usual allies to turn  to chief ropes two unwitting companions  into his fight the aforementioned weapon  who was supposed to delete herself all  in completion of her purpose and the  pilot a man desperately trying to just  get home to unsc space after being  stranded the first half of act 1 and  most of act 3 are traditional halo  contained linear levels where you're  guided through gauntlets of enemies the  rest of the game incorporates a hybrid  open world you're able to freely move  through a wide open area and pursue  optional side missions or beeline for  the next main story objective reaching  those objectives typically funnels you  back into the more traditional halo  level design only for chief to emerge  into the open world again once the  mission is complete in this way infinite  maintains the cinematic quality of  halo's traditionally linear campaigns  while also incorporating an open world  to explore those optional side missions  bring aspects of halo's multiplayer into  infinite's campaign creating scenarios  in which players can approach a problem  in numerous ways each of which can lead  to satisfying and occasionally hilarious  outcomes experimentation is often  rewarded with satisfying victory or a  story of how everything went hilariously  wrong regardless of the approach taken  it's fun to dismantle the banished  encampments on zeta halo wrestling  control away from them slowly but surely  even on infinite's harder difficulties  you can easily skip over all the  optional missions and still get that  linear halo experience if you want but  infinite provides compelling reasons for  stepping off the main path in a first  for halo there's an in-game progression  system in the campaign completing tasks  like saving captured unsc soldiers and  destroying banished roadblocks or  refineries nets you valor which unlocks  new vehicles and weapons that can be  summoned at forward operating bases  across the map allowing you to bring  your favorite loadouts into future side  quests and story missions you can also  find spartan cores which are spent to  upgrade chief's equipment from a  gameplay perspective the grapple shot is  the true star of infinite's campaign  though there are plenty of firearms  melee weapons and grenades for chief to  use and each tears through enemies with  a nice kinetic punch the grapple shot  fundamentally changes how you play a  halo game for the better in terms of  traversal a grappling hook makes chief  more nimble and opens up new vertical  pathways while exploring zeta halo but  the tool is far more valuable in combat  allowing you to grab far off firearms  when your current one runs out of ammo  pull you into range that chief can land  some solid hits or simply yank you out  of a firefight that's not going your way  it opens up brand new strategies for  handling old enemies too for instance  jackals will momentarily drop their  guard if you grapple and pull at their  shields  the grapple shot makes chief a faster  and more efficient killer you're still  doing the same loop of gunfire grenades  in melee and the dance of using your  shield to tank hits and taking cover  when said shield is broken is still the  same too but chief can storm through  enemies far more efficiently now adding  more strategic considerations to the  tried and true formula chief's remaining  equipment doesn't quite reach the same  highs as the grapple shot but they are  still valuable in combat each in their  own way enemies hit hard in the heroic  and legendary difficulties and mastering  how to use all of juice equipment opens  up new means of taking them on and  surviving oftentimes it can feel like  infinite is actively discouraging you  from engaging in the other equipment  though the grapple shot is unbelievably  useful in practically every situation  you have to temporarily switch away from  it to use chief's other equipment this  does lessen the impact of unlocking a  new ability as it's hard to get excited  over new equipment but i know i'm likely  only going to use it a few times that i  can keep the grapple shot ready in my  fight against the banished  the master chief returns  we thought you did  tossed  the war chief of the banished isn't all  that interesting as a principal  antagonist the dude's got an incredible  i am clearly the bad guy voice that  reverberates with a growling rumble  whenever he monologues but his  motivations don't really extend beyond  being the leader of a group of  bloodthirsty killers prior to the events  of infinite the banished wage to  months-long war on the unesc that saw  their leader psychologically and  physically dismantle the human military  soldiers and spartans but none of that  absolutely evil energy is present in  infinite resulting in a villain who's  hard to care about instead the  intriguing characterization is reserved  for chief the weapon and the pilot the  trio form a family unit with chief at  the head this setup affords some  incredible insight into chief psyche as  the armored warriors confronted with two  allies who aren't career soldiers a  truly incredible scene partway through  the game sees the pilot and chief  confront each other and what follows is  a rare moment of vulnerability that  humanizes chief in an unexpected and  meaningful way scenes like this are  sprinkled throughout the campaign as  chief the pilot and the weapon all  confront their own humanity and their  innate responsibility to each other one  moment in particular however does not  land part way through infinite chief  takes away the weapon's agency in an  incredibly problematic way the weapon is  understandably upset at the betrayal and  lashes out of chief's callousness only  for her to quickly soften and forgive  chief upon learning his motivations it  feels like a rushed development and one  that unfortunately implies that if they  have a good reason dads have a  responsibility to decide a child's  entire life the second said child steps  out of line the whole thing isn't  resolved in a great way while the  quality of writing may occasionally dip  each key scene is always bolstered by  infinite superb soundtrack the nature of  the hybrid open world means that most of  infinite's major story beats and the  conversations between characters occur  in set locations and so the orchestral  score can be carefully timed with the  game's most poignant moments there has  yet to be a mainline halo game with  anything less than an absolute banger of  a musical score and infinite does not  buck that trend the rich soundtrack is  helped along by the equally impressive  level of detail through for the  industries has achieved with facial  expressions on xbox series x having a  rousing score accompanied by believable  acting and expressions means there is a  collection of genuinely epic and tragic  moments in infinite that really land  halo infinite strives to transform what  it means to be a halo game making chief  into a reluctant father figure for a  young and naive ai and putting him into  an open world setting turns out that was  a risk worth taking for the franchise as  infinite is an incredible game there are  flaws but this is the best halo campaign  in years certain story elements are on  the weaker side and the amazing grapple  shot makes the rest of chief's equipment  feel a bit lacking in comparison but  these are small shortcomings what  otherwise feels like the right step for  halo to take going  forward eyes up reinforcements
 

